/**
 * The graph of a function, as a value. A function is a graph; this module reads it and, in a
 * draft, edits it. The handles `Graph`, `Node`, `Op`, `TypeRef`, `Region` and `Tag` name things
 * inside the graphs of one function and compare with `==` and `!=`. Reads are pure.
 *
 * A draft is a `Graph`: `open(f)` answers the graph `f` is, in a copy the edits go to, and every
 * handle read through it names a node of that copy. The root queries take the draft -- `nodes(d)`,
 * `graphs(d)`, `params(d)`, `exit(d)` -- and an edit names the nodes it changes and nothing else:
 * `replace(old, new)`, `erase(n)`. The draft is reached through the nodes, so a second argument
 * naming it is not needed and not offered (docs/design/graph-editing/README.cn.md 15.2, 15.3).
 *
 * A pass is a function from a function to a function of the same type, `(f: F) => F!`, so it
 * applies to one function, `loss->opt::fold`, or to the program's entry on the pipeline,
 * `camel prog.cml opt::fold std::nvm`. A pass written in Camel opens a draft on `f` (`open`),
 * replaces and erases nodes, and commits (`commit`), which gives the changed function: the same
 * type, the captured values it had, and every graph nothing changed shared. What a pass reads of
 * the program around the function (declarations, tags, facts) is read through the function; no
 * snapshot of the program is in this interface.
 *
 * A module that holds passes is compiled as a program of its own, whose entry is the pass: its
 * module-level values are computed before the pass runs. It may import modules written in C++ and
 * libraries written in Camel (for example `array`); the imports are declared and built with it.
 */

// The declarations of the operators of the C++ module `lib/graph` (design principles 6.4):
// the C++ side keeps the kernels, the executor and the handles. Every signature is stated, so the
// module has no computed signatures and no `graph.cml` under `lib/graph/signatures/`: a function-valued
// argument is `F` with `when is_function::<F>()`.
//
// `operator ==` is declared as the reserved group `__eq__`. `operator !=` cannot be declared
// (design principles 4.3: an overload of `==` gives it, and `operator !=` is
// OperatorNotOverloadable), and a declared `==` is negated by the compiler -- the graph holds
// this declaration and the negation -- so no entry in `__neq__` is needed for the handle types.

import { is_function, is_ordered } from types

/// The types of the module are provided by it (design principles 10.4); a handle names a thing
/// inside the graphs of one function and compares with `==` and `!=`.
export type Graph
export type Node<T>
export type Region
export type TypeRef
export type Op
export type Tag
export type NodeKind
export type RegionKind
export type Effects
export type Value = Node

// ---- the two readonly resources (design principles 9.5, 9.12; design 15.5) ----
// Reading the graph a function is, and reading its spelling (a name, a source position, the number
// it was created with), are accesses to a representation the program does not write: declared here
// so that a clause names them with `uses graph` and `uses spelling`, inferred rather than written
// at most call sites. A `readonly` resource is only ever read -- `uses var graph` is refused -- and
// two accesses to it conflict with nothing and produce no order.

/// The graph a function is, read (design principles 9.12).
export resource graph readonly

/// The spelling of the program: names, source positions, creation numbers (design principles 9.5).
export resource spelling readonly

// ---- the graphs of a function ----

/// The graph of a function value: its body.
/// @example graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1)
export func graph_of::<F>(f: F) => Graph when is_function::<F>()

/// The graph of a function value and every graph it calls, directly or not, in the order of
/// their ids; each graph once.
/// @example len(graphs_of((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func graphs_of::<F>(f: F) => Graph[] when is_function::<F>()

// ---- the draft and the root queries ----
// The first argument of each query is the draft `d` a block binds, and is the same `Graph` a
// function value's own graph is: a query reads whichever it is handed, and an edit changes the
// copy the draft is. The names are the ones docs/design/graph-editing/README.cn.md 15.2 settled.

/// The nodes of the graph in canonical order: the order the graph's own structure decides, from the
/// exit and the regions' outer accesses along the data and sync edges in port order, with what the
/// ports leave open arranged for the smallest numbering table (design principles 9.5, the same walk
/// `same_graph` and the block naming use). Two programs that differ only in what the source spelled
/// -- two statements swapped, a variable renamed -- give one order, so a pass may pair neighbours in
/// it and get one answer for both. The places of removed nodes are skipped; a node nothing reaches
/// from the exit (a pure computation nobody uses) comes after the ones that are reached.
/// A draft has no canonical order -- its places are the order its nodes were made in, and the
/// commit replaces them -- so a draft answers its own order, and `id` of one of its nodes is
/// refused rather than answered with a number that says nothing.
/// @example nodes(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func nodes(d: Graph) => Node[]

/// The graphs of the draft, `d` included: every graph the function of `d` calls, directly or not,
/// each once, in the order of their ids. Each is the draft of that graph, so a node read through
/// one of them is a node an edit changes.
/// @example len(graphs(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1)))
export func graphs(d: Graph) => Graph[]

/// The parameter ports of the graph, in the order the declaration states them: the nodes the body
/// takes its parameters as. A port is where an edit adds a parameter (`add_param`), and it is the
/// node a caller's argument is read into.
/// @example len(params(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1)))
export func params(d: Graph) => Node[]

/// The node the graph hands its value out of: the node a graph returns, the port of a function that
/// returns a parameter, or the `JOIN` a body's branches end at. It is the one anchor an edit that
/// changes what the function gives has to name, since `replace` refuses an anchor: read this node,
/// build the value that takes its place, and `replace` this node by that value. Null for a graph
/// that hands no value out (a function of `void`), which has no node to put anything in the place
/// of.
/// @example exit(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func exit(d: Graph) => Node?

// ---- reading nodes ----

/// The nodes whose values `n` takes as ordinary inputs, in order.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example inputs(n)
export func inputs(n: Node) => Node[]

/// The nodes whose values `n` takes as with inputs.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example with_inputs(n)
export func with_inputs(n: Node) => Node[]

/// The nodes that must run before `n` when both run, because a sync block puts them in one chain
/// (the sync edges of design principles 3.1).
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example sync_inputs(n)
export func sync_inputs(n: Node) => Node[]

/// The nodes that must run before `n` when both run, because they use the same resource (the
/// order edges of design principles 3.1); an order edge never makes a node run.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example order_inputs(n)
export func order_inputs(n: Node) => Node[]

/// The nodes that read `n` as an input of any kind, once each, in NodeId order.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example users(n)
export func users(n: Node) => Node[]

/// The nodes that read the value of `n` (ordinary or with input), once each.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example len(value_users(n)) == 0
export func value_users(n: Node) => Node[]

/// The kind of the node.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example kind(n) == NodeKind::Oper
export func kind(n: Node) => NodeKind

/// The operator of an operation node, null for any other node.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example op(n)
export func op(n: Node) => Op?

/// The URI of the operator, such as `:op/add_l`.
/// @example-fragment `o` is an operator taken from a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example op_name(o)
export func op_name(o: Op) => string

/// Whether the node is an operation whose operator is declared pure.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example is_pure(n)
export func is_pure(n: Node) => bool

/// Whether a pass may compute the node's value itself and treat it as a value: its operator is
/// pure *and* foldable, so folding it, merging it with an identical call or dropping it when
/// nothing reads it cannot change the program. A pure operator that must run where the program
/// writes it (a rule's value, a `fact`) is not one, and neither is any node that is no operation.
/// This is the question the C++ fold, cse and dce ask (`OperatorTable::mayBeRecomputed`); whether
/// the call may also be *moved* or dropped asks `may_fault` as well.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example may_be_recomputed(n)
export func may_be_recomputed(n: Node) => bool

/// Whether running the node can raise a fault where the program writes it (a division by zero, an
/// index out of range, a conversion whose value does not fit). It is the operator table's own fact
/// (`OperatorTraits::mayFault`, the one the C++ sweep reads) and the conversion's (`castMayFault`);
/// a call, a loop or a gate nobody declared answers true, and an elementwise operator whose
/// operands' shapes the types state in full answers false. A node that may fault is not a value a
/// pass may drop, move or merge away, whatever the purity of its operator.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example may_fault(n)
export func may_fault(n: Node) => bool

/// Whether a pass may arrange the node anywhere: drop it, move it, merge it with an identical
/// call, or leave it with no order edge after an inline. The node must be pure *and* unable to
/// fault (design principles 3.4); a pure operator that must run where the program writes it, one
/// that may fault, one nobody declared, a call, a loop or a gate are not free. This is the one
/// predicate behind that question on both sides of the boundary: `core.arrangeable`
/// (`camel/facts/arrangeable.h`, `nodeIsFreelyArrangeable`) is the fact it reads, the C++ passes
/// ask that fact, and a pass written in Camel reads this name. Ask it rather than writing
/// `is_pure(n) && may_be_recomputed(n) && !may_fault(n)`: the two writings drift apart.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example may_be_rearranged(n)
export func may_be_rearranged(n: Node) => bool

/// Whether the node anchors its graph (its exit, output or return, or the end of a branch arm); a
/// transaction does not replace or erase it.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example is_anchor(n)
export func is_anchor(n: Node) => bool

/// Whether the value of the node may be written in place: it is passed to a `var` parameter or to
/// an operator that changes its operand, directly or through gates. A rewrite must not treat such
/// a node as one value.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example is_written_slot(n)
export func is_written_slot(n: Node) => bool

/// Whether the node is a constant of the graph.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example is_constant(n)
export func is_constant(n: Node) => bool

/// The bytes the node's constant value occupies: the measure `std::opt` and `std::cxx::fold` clamp with
/// `constant_budget`, so that a pass written in Camel can ask the same question the native pass asks. The
/// cost is the value's elements plus the shape that describes them, the walk follows members 8 deep, and a
/// node that is no constant costs 0.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example constant_bytes(n)
export func constant_bytes(n: Node) => int
/// The type of the node's value, null when it has none.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example type_of(n)
export func type_of(n: Node) => TypeRef?

/// The type as a program writes it.
/// @example-fragment t is the type of a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example type_name(t)    // 'int'
export func type_name(t: TypeRef) => string

/// The graph a call or function node names, null for any other node, and null for a call whose
/// function value names no graph -- a parameter of function type, a closure being assembled: which
/// graph runs is decided when the call runs. A call written by name answers through the function
/// node it reads, and a call through a value through the function value the graph holds. So the
/// call a program's graph makes answers the graph of the program's entry function, which is how a
/// pass reaches the body it is there to rewrite without naming a graph (design principles 9.5).
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example callee(n)
export func callee(n: Node) => Graph?

/// The attributes of the node as text (empty when there are none).
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example tags(n)
export func tags(n: Node) => string

// ---- the tags a declaration writes ----
// A tag whose argument is a function (`#jvp(rule)`, `#transpose(rule)`, `#vjp(rule)`) is a fact
// about the declaration a pass needs the function of, not text about it: `declaration_tags` reads
// it back by structure, and `rule_of` names the one a transform asks for.

/// The tags written on the declaration of the function of this graph whose argument is a
/// function, in the order of their names; empty for a graph that is no declaration (a lambda) and
/// for a declaration that writes none. The declaration's facts travel with the graph, so a pass
/// reads them without the module that declares the function.
/// @example declaration_tags(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func declaration_tags(g: Graph) => Tag[]

/// The name of the tag as the program writes it (`jvp`, `vjp`); the qualified name when the tag is
/// not declared.
/// @example-fragment t is a tag read from a declaration
/// @example tag_name(t)    // 'jvp'
export func tag_name(t: Tag) => string

/// The graphs the tag's arguments name, in the order written: `#jvp(mul_jvp)` names one.
/// @example-fragment t is a tag read from a declaration
/// @example tag_graphs(t)
export func tag_graphs(t: Tag) => Graph[]

/// The rule the declaration of `g` writes for the transformation `name`: `jvp` is forward mode,
/// `transpose` the rule of a linear function, and `vjp` the rule that gives the pullback directly.
/// Null when the declaration writes none, when it writes that tag twice, and when what the tag
/// names is not a function. The name is the one the program writes, or its qualified spelling.
/// @example rule_of(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1), 'vjp')
export func rule_of(g: Graph, name: string) => Graph?

// ---- reading spelling ----
// The reads of 15.2 whose answer is *spelling*: the name the source wrote for a graph, where a node
// was written, the number a handle was created with. Each needs its declaration to write
// `spelling` (design principles 9.5); a transform that would pick a function by its name takes the
// function value instead.

/// The name of the graph. This is *spelling*: it is the identifier the source wrote, so reading it
/// needs a declaration that writes `spelling` in its clause (design principles 9.5). A transform
/// that would pick a function by its name takes the function value instead.
/// @example-fragment the reading needs a declaration that writes spelling in its clause, and a doc example is a body with nowhere to write one (design principles 9.5)
/// @example name(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func name(g: Graph) => string spelling

/// The source origin id of the node, 0 when unknown. This is *spelling*: it is where the node was
/// written, so reading it needs a declaration that writes `spelling` in its clause (design
/// principles 9.5). Diagnostics and the debugger declare it; a transform does not read it.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example-fragment the reading needs a declaration that writes spelling in its clause, and a doc example is a body with nowhere to write one (design principles 9.5)
/// @example origin(n)
export func origin(n: Node) => int spelling

/// The value of a constant node as the type T (a number, a bool or a string), null for a node
/// that is no constant of that type. T is written (`constant::<int>(n)`).
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example constant::<int>(n)
export func constant::<T>(n: Node) => T? when is_ordered::<T>()

// ---- regions ----

/// The innermost region the node is in. Every node is in one: region 0 is the body, also in a
/// graph whose region table is empty.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example region_of(n)
export func region_of(n: Node) => Region

/// The kind of the region: `Root` (the function body, region 0), `Arm`, `LoopCond`, `LoopBody`,
/// `Inline`, `Block` or `Dead`.
/// @example-fragment r is a region of the surrounding pass
/// @example region_kind(r) == RegionKind::Inline
export func region_kind(r: Region) => RegionKind

/// The region the region is in; null for the body (region 0).
/// @example-fragment r is a region of the surrounding pass
/// @example region_parent(r)
export func region_parent(r: Region) => Region?

/// The node on one line: its id, kind, operator and type. The text is *spelling*: it shows the
/// number the node was created with, so reading it needs a declaration that writes `spelling` in
/// its clause (design principles 9.5). Diagnostics and the debugger declare it.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example-fragment the reading needs a declaration that writes spelling in its clause, and a doc example is a body with nowhere to write one (design principles 9.5)
/// @example show(n)    // '%2 Oper :op/mul_l : int'
export func show(n: Node) => string spelling

/// The canonical number of the node: its position in the order `nodes` gives, which the graph's own
/// structure decides (design principles 9.5). Two programs that differ only in what the source
/// spelled -- the order two statements were written in, the names a variable was given -- number
/// their nodes the same, so a pass may key a map by this number and compare two of them to ask which
/// node comes first in the canonical order. A node of a draft has no canonical number at all, and
/// reading one is refused with the fault `InvalidArgument` of `graph.id`: the position a node holds
/// while the draft is open is the order the nodes were made in, and the commit replaces it, so an
/// answer there would be a number that changes. Read `id` after the commit publishes the program, or
/// read `creation_id` (which declares `spelling`) for the draft's own order.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example id(n)
#op('graph:id')
export func id(h: Node) => int

/// The number a graph, a node or an operator was created with inside its program: the GraphId, the
/// NodeId or the OperId. It is stable for the program, and it is *spelling*: it says the order the
/// program was written in, which a transform may not depend on. Reading it needs a declaration that
/// writes `spelling` in its clause (design principles 9.5); to order two nodes, use `id`.
/// @example-fragment `n` is a graph, a node or an operator of the surrounding pass
/// @example creation_id(n)
#op('graph:creation_id')
export func creation_id(h: Graph) => int spelling
#op('graph:creation_id')
export func creation_id(h: Node) => int spelling
#op('graph:creation_id')
export func creation_id(h: Op) => int spelling

// ---- facts ----

/// What running the graph once does besides computing its value: `external`, `writes_variables`,
/// `may_fault`, `may_not_terminate`.
/// @example effects(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1)).external
export func effects(g: Graph) => Effects

/// The shape of the node's value as text, null when its value is no array of known element type.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example shape_text(n)    // 'f32 [2, 3]'
export func shape_text(n: Node) => string?

/// The numeric permissions the program granted the node, as a bit set (0 for none).
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example permissions(n)
export func permissions(n: Node) => int

/// How dear one run of the node is, as its operator states it in the operator's record: a division
/// states more than a multiplication and a multiplication more than an addition, and a node whose
/// operator states nothing (a datum, a port, an operator nobody priced) reads as the cheapest run,
/// 1.0. This is the reading a cost function makes instead of comparing the operator's name: the
/// number is the operator owner's statement, and every cost function that reads it agrees on which
/// of two writings is dearer (design principles 9.11, "the cost of an extraction").
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding pass
/// @example relative_cost(n)
export func relative_cost(n: Node) => float

// ---- the draft ----
// The edits of the table of docs/design/graph-editing/README.cn.md 15.3, in its own spellings: the
// draft is not an argument. `open` hands the draft over as the `Graph` it is, an edit names the
// nodes it changes, and every handle read through the draft names a node of the copy the edits go
// to -- so a node is enough to say which draft, and `replace(old, new)` is the whole of it. A node
// of a published graph is refused by the fault each operation states: an edit changes a draft, and
// a program is immutable (design principles 3.3).

/// Opens a function's graphs as a draft and gives the graph of the function itself: what an edit
/// changes and what the root queries read. Nothing changes until `commit`, which gives the changed
/// function; the draft is reached through the handles read from it, so it is passed to no edit.
/// @example let d = open((x: int): int => x + 1)
export func open::<F>(f: F) => Graph when is_function::<F>()

/// Puts the node `new` of the same graph in the place of `old`, which is removed; what `old`
/// ordered stays ordered. Both nodes are of the draft, and `new` is normally a node `eval` or
/// `add_constant` answered.
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when a node anchors its graph, when `new` is computed from `old`, when a node is of a published graph, or when the draft is over
/// @example-fragment n and its only input are nodes of the surrounding draft
/// @example replace(n, inputs(n)[0])
export func replace(old: Node, new: Node) => void

/// Removes a node whose value nobody reads.
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when the value is read, when the node anchors its graph, when the node is of a published graph, or when the draft is over
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding draft
/// @example erase(n)
export func erase(n: Node) => void

/// Evaluates the pure operator of the node over its constant operands and gives the node holding
/// the result, null when there is no value to compute (an operand is no constant of the graph, the
/// node is no pure operation, or evaluating it faults). The node the answer names is the draft's to
/// put in place of `n` with `replace`. A constant expression that can only fault is left as written
/// and warned about, as the C++ fold does.
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding draft
/// @example eval(n)
export func eval(n: Node) => Node?

/// Adds an ordering to the graph: when both nodes run, `before` runs before `after`. An order edge
/// only orders and never makes a node run (the order edges of design principles 3.1), so a
/// computation that has to happen where it is written is put in a `sync` block instead; this is how
/// a pass gives a node the place it needs among the accesses of one resource. Both nodes must be of
/// one graph, and the edge is refused when the graph already computes `before` from `after`, which
/// is the cycle it would close. Ordering two nodes that are ordered that way already changes
/// nothing and is not an error.
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when the two nodes are not of one graph, when either is gone, when either is of a published graph, or when the edge would form a cycle
/// @example-fragment a and b are nodes of the surrounding draft
/// @example order(a, b)
export func order(before: Node, after: Node) => void

/// Splices the body of the function value `fn` into the region `at` is in and gives the node that
/// holds the body's value. `at` decides the place as well: a statement of its block's chain (a
/// call, an effect, a node that may fault) hands that place over to the code the body becomes, and
/// the statement is gone afterwards -- what it held is read from the body's value; a node that
/// stands in no chain only selects the region, is left as it is, and the ordering of the body's
/// writes among the accesses already there is `order`'s to say. `fn` takes no arguments, and only a
/// function value that captures no variable can be expanded.
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when the function value captures variables, when the node anchors its graph (`at` standing in a chain), when the body cannot be spliced, when `at` is of a published graph, or when the draft is over
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding draft
/// @example inline(n, () => 0)
export func inline::<G>(at: Node, fn: G) => Node when is_function::<G>()

/// Collects a group of the graph's nodes into a function of its own and puts a call of it where the
/// value the group handed on was read: `inline`'s reverse (design 7 and 15.3). The nodes the group
/// reads from outside it become that function's parameters, in the order they are found, and the one
/// value the group hands on becomes its result; the call the operator answers reads those same
/// values, and its callee (`callee`) is the function the group became. The group's own nodes are
/// gone from the graph afterwards, so the code stands there once.
///
/// A group is a segment with a clear boundary: operations only, whose values come in from outside it
/// and go out through one node, with no anchor of the graph in it, no access, no ordering and no
/// place in a chain. A group that is not that is refused with the rule it broke, before anything of
/// it is copied.
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when the group is empty, when its nodes are not all of one graph of the draft, when one of them anchors its graph, when one of them is no pure operation, when the group hands on no value or more than one, or when the draft is over
/// @example-fragment ns is a group of the surrounding draft's nodes
/// @example outline(ns)
export func outline(ns: Node[]) => Node

/// Adds a parameter to the draft's graph and gives the port node it is: a node of that graph's own
/// body, of the same type as `like`, whose value is what a caller passes. A parameter is two
/// statements about one thing -- this node, which the body computes with, and the entry of the
/// function type a caller is told about -- and this operator writes the first alone: the second is
/// the declaration, and the block head that carries a new signature is what writes it
/// (docs/design/graph-editing/README.cn.md 8.4). Until that head exists the declaration is written
/// by a caller that states the widened signature for itself, and a commit of a graph whose port list
/// is one entry longer than its signature is refused, naming the port node.
///
/// The type is the type of a node and not a name: a graph edited through this interface says what
/// its new parameter is by naming a value of that type, which is the statement a caller can make
/// today (`type_of` gives a `TypeRef?`, and nothing in the language turns one into a `TypeRef`).
/// The block form of 15.3 states it in the head instead, `add_param(d)`, and needs no argument
/// here.
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when `like` is not a node of the graph the draft edits, when it has no type, or when the draft is over
/// @example-fragment n is a node of the surrounding draft
/// @example add_param(n)
export func add_param(like: Node) => Node

/// Adds a value to the graph's static values and gives the node that holds it. The value is a
/// number, a bool or a string; a string is copied into the permanent space because a value a draft
/// creates may be live at a safepoint where its draft is not traced.
/// @example add_constant(graph_of((x: int): int => x + 1), 7)
export func add_constant::<T>(g: Graph, value: T) => Node when is_ordered::<T>()

/// Checks the edits and makes the changed function: the same type as the one that was opened, with
/// the values it captured, sharing every graph nothing changed. The draft is over.
/// @fails error value `GraphConflict` when a graph the draft touched changed since it was opened; error value `GraphInvalid` when an edit broke an invariant (the message names the graph, the node and the edge); error value `TxnUsed` when the draft was over already
/// @example commit(open((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func commit::<F>(d: Graph) => F!

/// Drops the draft.
/// @example abort(open((x: int): int => x + 1))
export func abort(d: Graph) => void

// ---- comparison ----
// `==` of the handle types (design principles 4.3): a library defines the operators of its own
// types. Two handles are equal when they name the same thing of the same function.

#op('graph:eq')
export func operator ==(a: Graph, b: Graph) => bool
#op('graph:eq')
export func operator ==(a: Node, b: Node) => bool
#op('graph:eq')
export func operator ==(a: Region, b: Region) => bool
#op('graph:eq')
export func operator ==(a: TypeRef, b: TypeRef) => bool
#op('graph:eq')
export func operator ==(a: Op, b: Op) => bool
#op('graph:eq')
export func operator ==(a: Tag, b: Tag) => bool

/// The self-test of two checks that answer "a node the program has": makes a handle that claims a
/// node the program does not have and reports whether the claim was refused where the handle was
/// made; then asks `nodeRegion` of the same wrong ref, after printing the body region of a node
/// that is there. It is a test, in no pipeline, and naming it is the whole switch: `HANDLE_PROBE
/// allowed a node that is there` (the control), `HANDLE_PROBE refused: ...` for the node that is
/// not, then `REGION_PROBE body region of node 0: 0` and, instead of a third answer, the abort the
/// region guard raises -- 0 is the body, so a node that is not there has no region to be given.
export func probe_handle::<F>(f: F) => F! when is_function::<F>()

/// Explicitly checks standard ambient resource/access-mode support and matched effect ordering
/// between two functions, plus the normal port, entry/exit and effect-credential admission checks.
/// Returns `after` on success. This comparison is not an observational-equivalence proof:
/// equal support does not count events or compare their payloads. The selected check emits a
/// located context diagnostic on refusal; catching the error value does not recover outer
/// execution. Ordinary invocation and CLI wrappers use this same checking stage.
/// @fails error value `PassBoundary` when the selected boundary check refuses
/// @example check_pass_boundary
export func check_pass_boundary::<F>(before: F, after: F) => F! when is_function::<F>()
